<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- MeArm digital-twin scene, adapted 2026-09-24 from zc110747/MeArmPilot
     (MeArm-3D/simulation/mujoco/mearm.xml, MIT license,
     https://github.com/zc110747/MeArmPilot), not built from scratch --
     see SESSION_LOG.md's 2026-09-23/24 entries for why: this project's
     Phase 4 goal is a MEArm digital twin used as a preview/visualization
     tool (so live control logic can be sanity-checked without depending
     on real, currently power-flaky hardware), and MeArmPilot already had
     a complete, working, self-contained (no external mesh files -- only
     primitive box/cylinder/capsule geoms) model built the same way this
     project builds things: their own README states joint geometry was
     derived from "camera photos + a degree-by-degree servo sweep" against
     a REAL physical unit, and that their own initial shoulder/elbow role
     assumption (from firmware naming) turned out to be WRONG until they
     checked -- independent confirmation of the exact kind of mixup this
     project also hit and fixed the same way (see SESSION_LOG's 2026-09-21
     entry on confirming channel 2 = shoulder).

     IMPORTANT CAVEAT, not yet resolved: the <joint>/<actuator> range=/
     ctrlrange= values below are MeArmPilot's OWN measured numbers, from
     THEIR physical MeArm unit -- not this project's jin-hua clone kit.
     This project's own real measured ranges are in pulse_us
     (servo_limit_finder_4ch, base=500-2500/shoulder=1200-2100/
     elbow=500-1850/claw=1300-1600us, see phase3_control_loop_main.cpp's
     2026-09-23 comment), and there is no verified pulse-us-to-radian
     conversion factor for this project's own specific servos/joints to
     convert those into a matching set of radian ranges here. Until that
     conversion is established, treat this file's joint ranges as a
     reasonable stand-in for a PREVIEW tool, not a geometrically accurate
     twin of this project's own physical arm -- consistent with this
     model's stated purpose (see the scene comment above).

     Renamed from MeArmPilot's own arbitrary Arduino-pin-based actuator
     names (servo_9/servo_7/servo_8/servo_6) to this project's own
     base/shoulder/elbow/claw convention (matches phase3_control_loop_
     main.cpp's TIM3 channel comments and README.md's Phase 4 section) --
     no other structural change from the upstream file, including its
     tendon+equality trick for keeping the claw level (replaces actually
     modeling MeArm's real parallel-linkage rigid bodies -- a real MeArm's
     claw orientation is a mechanical CONSEQUENCE of the shoulder+elbow
     linkage geometry, not an independent DOF; MeArmPilot's own upstream
     comment confirms this "tool" joint is passive, kept in the model only
     to make that geometric consequence visible, not something this
     project's control code should ever try to actuate directly). -->
<mujoco model="mearm">
  <compiler angle="radian" autolimits="true"/>
  <option timestep="0.001" integrator="implicitfast" solver="Newton" iterations="80" tolerance="1e-10" gravity="0 0 -9.81"/>
  <default>
    <geom friction="1 0.005 0.0001" solref="0.02 1" solimp="0.9 0.95 0.001" margin="0.001" condim="3" density="0"/>
    <default class="world">
      <geom contype="1" conaffinity="3" group="1" density="0"/>
    </default>
    <default class="visual">
      <geom contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2" density="0" condim="1" margin="0" solref="0.02 1"/>
    </default>
    <default class="collision">
      <geom contype="2" conaffinity="3" group="3" density="0"/>
    </default>
    <default class="arm">
      <joint type="hinge" damping="0.01" frictionloss="0.005" armature="0.0005" limited="true"/>
      <!-- 2026-09-24: forcerange widened from MeArmPilot's own upstream
           0.1765 (their real measured servo torque spec) to 2.0. Found via
           a real settle-and-measure test (not assumed): with the original
           value, commanding shoulder to its own range extreme (e.g. 0.898)
           settled far short (~0.66) even with the table collision below
           already fixed -- not enough simulated torque to hold the arm out
           against gravity at that angle. This project's own stated use for
           this model is a rough live PREVIEW (see this file's top header
           comment), not a torque-realistic twin, so trading that realism
           away to guarantee the commanded angle is actually reached is the
           right tradeoff here -- a real MEArm's real SG92R has its own
           real torque limit, already handled by servo_limit_finder_4ch on
           the actual hardware, not by this simulation. -->
      <position kp="5" kv="0.5" forcerange="-2.0 2.0"/>
    </default>
  </default>
  <worldbody>
    <light name="key_light" pos="0.3 0.3 0.6" dir="-0.5 -0.5 -1" diffuse="0.8 0.8 0.8" specular="0.2 0.2 0.2"/>
    <light name="fill_light" pos="-0.3 -0.2 0.4" dir="0.5 0.3 -1" diffuse="0.35 0.35 0.35"/>
    <geom name="floor" class="world" type="plane" size="0.6 0.6 0.01" pos="0 0 0" rgba="0.45 0.47 0.5 1"/>
    <!-- 2026-09-24: table_top's collision disabled (contype/conaffinity=0
         override, below) after a real settle-and-measure test found the
         claw physically colliding with it (a real MjData.contact, not
         assumed) whenever shoulder swung toward its own reaching-forward
         range end -- blocking the arm from ever reaching a real commanded
         angle, not a control/calibration issue. This table's position was
         inherited as-is from MeArmPilot's own upstream scene (see this
         file's top comment) and was never re-checked against this
         project's own real target-object placement (still an open,
         deliberately-deferred decision -- see SESSION_LOG.md). Kept
         visually (as a size/scale reference) but non-colliding until that
         placement decision is actually made. -->
    <body name="table" pos="0.17 0 0.031">
      <geom name="table_top" class="world" type="box" size="0.11 0.11 0.015" rgba="0.62 0.55 0.42 1"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
    </body>
    <body name="base_link" pos="0 0 0">
